name: processes
intro: Every program you run was started by another one. A shell makes a
intro: new process with `fork`, and that process turns into your command
intro: with `exec`. Put the POSIX line first, and include <unistd.h> and
intro: <sys/wait.h>.
intro:
intro:     pid_t pid = fork();
intro:
intro: `fork` copies the running program -- memory, open files, everything
intro: -- and returns twice. In the original (the parent) it returns the
intro: child's pid; in the copy (the child) it returns 0; on failure it
intro: returns -1 and there is no child. Both processes carry on from the
intro: same line.
intro:
intro:     if (pid == 0) {
intro:         execvp(argv[1], argv + 1);   /* returns only on failure */
intro:         _exit(127);
intro:     }
intro:     int status;
intro:     waitpid(pid, &status, 0);
intro:
intro: `execvp(file, argv)` replaces the child with another program. It
intro: looks `file` up on PATH; `argv` is a NULL-ended array of strings
intro: whose first item is the program's own name. If it returns at all, it
intro: failed.
intro:
intro: Why `_exit` and not `exit`? `exit` flushes stdio buffers. The child
intro: holds a copy of whatever the parent printed but had not flushed yet,
intro: so the text comes out twice. `_exit` leaves at once and flushes
intro: nothing. The rule: a child that does not exec leaves with `_exit`,
intro: and a parent that printed `fflush(stdout)`s before it forks.
intro:
intro: `waitpid(pid, &status, 0)` waits for that child and collects it; a
intro: child nobody collects lingers as a zombie. `status` is packed bits
intro: -- never print it raw. Ask it:
intro:
intro:     WIFEXITED(status)     then WEXITSTATUS(status) is the exit code
intro:     WIFSIGNALED(status)   then WTERMSIG(status) is the signal number
intro:
intro: A child killed by a signal has no exit code. Plain `wait` takes
intro: whichever child ends first, which is not repeatable. `waitpid` with
intro: a pid takes the one you name, so you can report in the order you
intro: started them.
---
q: twoways.c -- fork, and tell the two apart
spec: Print `before`, then `fork`. The child prints `child`, flushes it, and
spec: exits with status 7. The parent waits for it, then prints
spec: `parent: child exited with 7` (the 7 comes from the child's real
spec: status) and exits 0. Each line appears exactly once, in that order.
spec:
spec: Careful: when standard output is a file or a pipe, text printed
spec: before the fork but not flushed is copied into the child.
spec:
spec: If `fork` fails print `twoways: fork failed` to standard error and
spec: exit 1.
file: twoways.c
prog: #define _POSIX_C_SOURCE 200809L
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: #include <stdlib.h>
prog: #include <sys/wait.h>
prog: #include <unistd.h>
prog: 
prog: int main(void)
prog: {
prog: 	printf("before\n");
prog: 	fflush(stdout);
prog: 	pid_t pid = fork();
prog: 	if (pid < 0) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "twoways: fork failed\n");
prog: 		return 1;
prog: 	}
prog: 	if (pid == 0) {
prog: 		printf("child\n");
prog: 		fflush(stdout);
prog: 		_exit(7);
prog: 	}
prog: 	int status;
prog: 	if (waitpid(pid, &status, 0) < 0) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "twoways: wait failed\n");
prog: 		return 1;
prog: 	}
prog: 	if (WIFEXITED(status))
prog: 		printf("parent: child exited with %d\n", WEXITSTATUS(status));
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: twoways
case: twoways; echo $?
case: twoways | cat
hint: fflush(stdout) before fork; in the child branch (pid == 0) print, fflush, _exit(7); in the parent waitpid, then WEXITSTATUS
